In California, the shark wounded a 13-year-old diver, who traded the lobster prey
According to official sources, on Saturday, the first day of lobster hunting season, a 13-year-old dive was attacked by a shark and was injured in California.
The attack occurred at about 7 a.m. not far from Beacon Beach in Encinitas.
According to Ched Hammel in an interview with KSW-TV (San Diego), on Saturday in the morning he dived with friends and after about half an hour heard the cries of the boy for help. Together with friends, they sailed on a boat to the site of the attack and pulled a teenager from the water.
According to Hammel, at first he decided that the boy rejoiced at catching the lobster loudly, but quickly “recognized that he was screaming “I was bitten!
I’ve been bitten!”
Swimming to the diver, Hammel noticed that "he was completely ruptured the scrubber around his collarbone."
“I crucifigated so that everyone would get out of the water, ‘Here is a Shark.’
The boy by helicopter was taken to the Children's Hospital Radi (San Diego); his condition is evaluated as critical.
The species of shark that attacked a teenager is unknown.
Rescuer Captain Larry Giles during a press briefing said that a few weeks ago a shark was spotted in this place, but she was not referred to dangerous species.
Gayles also added that the victim of the attack received injuries in the upper trunk area.
Officials have blocked the zone from Ponto Beach in Casablad to Swami beach in Encinitas for a 48-h-h-h-h-h-h-h-h-h-h-h-hang to investigate and protect the population.
Guils remarked that there are 135 species of sharks in this area, but most of them are not considered dangerous.
Seynsbury Plans to Conquer Perfume and Cosmetics of the UK
Seinsbury is going to establish cosmetics and perfumery departments in Boot stores, Superdrag and Debenhams with cosmetology consultants, based on a model of luxury department stores.
The perfumery-cosmetic UK market is valued at £2.8 bilglobby and continues to widen, while in the field of voice and household-saling goods are falling. To seize a significant share of this covet and perfumery market as an experiment in 11 stores around the country will be presented, spacious cosmetics and perfumery departments will be presented, and in the case of success of next year, they should be expected in many other stores.
Supermarket holders are looking for something to fill the shelrigs that were previously diluted for television, microwave ovens, household goods, and investments in the perfumery-cosmetial market seem to be a good solution.
According to representatives of Seinsbury, it is planned to mobilise an assortment of cosmetic products up to 3 thousand estimations; including for the first time such brands as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr. PavPaw will be offered.
Existing assemblies from L'Oreal, Maybelline and Burt's Bees will be unveiled on a larger scale, in specially designated places, such as product organization in Boot stores.
In addition, a proprietary line of Boutique Cosmetics products will be reorganized with the transition to vegan products to meet the needs of young buyers.
In the two Seynsbury stores will be presented kioks of perfümeral manufacturers Fragrance Shop: the first of them has already been opened in Kreudona in south London last week, and the opening of the second is expected in Selli Oak, Birmingham this year.
Online trading and the tendency to buy a small volume of products for every day in small shops near the house have led supermarkets to attract buyers in other ways.
Mike Cope, President of Seinsbury, says the outlets will gradually turn into department stores, and its supermarket chain is escaping for buyers competing against cheap stores like Aldi and Lidl, offering more services and non-food products.
Hundreds of Seinsbury supermarket supermarkets houses Argos, and in some of them you can now find Habitat stores since the network purchased both brands two years a year ago. This led to an increase in sales of food and revenues due to these acquisitions.
The supermarket's previous attempts to give a new life to their cosmetic and pharmacy departments were not successful.
In the beginning of the 2000-ies was organized a joint venture of Seinsbury and Boots, but in the future there was a disagreement between the participants in the division of income from the sales of pharmacy in supermarkets.
A new strategist was developed after the sale of Seynsbury Pharmaceutical Business (total 281 stores), Celesio, owner of Lloyds Pharmacsi, for £ 125 million three years a year earlier.
Lloyds is Expected to provide support in the implementation of the plan, adding a wide range of luxury skin care for La Roche Posay and Vichy in four stores.
Says Paul Mills-Hick, Commercial Director of Seinsbury: “We have completely transformed the appearance and atmosphere of our perfumery and cosmetics departments to create a more favorable environment for our customers.
We have also invited specially trained consultants, who will always be ready to help with advice.
Our assortment of stamps for every taste, pleasant design and convenient location of stores will attract a lot of visitors looking for cosmetics and perfumery. This will be a completely new concept of shopping."
