The Macedonians vote at a referendum about whether it is necessary to change the name of the country
The people of Macedonia on Sunday voted in a referendum on the question of whether to change the name of their country to the “Republic of North Macedonia”, which would allow to resolve a long-standing dispute with Greece, which blocked its applications for membership in the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which is part of the province of Macedonia, argues that the name of its northern neighbour is a claim to its territory and veto its accession to NATO and the EU.
In June, the two governments entered into an agreement based on proposition of a new name, but nationalist opponents argue that this change would undermine the ethnic identity of the Slavic majority of the population of Macedonia.
President Gheorghe Ivanov said that he will not vote in a referendum, and the boycott campaign has called into question the question of whether the voter turnout will be in accordance with the 50 per cent minimum required for the referendum.
The question put to a replication was formulated as follows: “Do you support the membership of NATO and the EU, subject to the adoption of an agreement with Greece?”
Proponents of the name, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, claim that Macedonia, one country that emerged after the dissolution of Yugoslavia, should pay this price for joining organizations such as the EU and NATO.
“I came today to vote for the future of the country, for the youth of Macedonia, so that they can live freely under the auspices of the European Union, because it means a safer life for all of us”, – says 79-year-old Oliver Georgievskaya, a resident of Skopje.
Sufficidalty a number of members of parliament said they would comply with the results of the vote in order to make it decisive, although it was not legally binding.
Modification of the name will require a two-thirds majority for members of parliament.
The state election commission said there were no reports of violations by 13:00.
However, the turnout accounted for only 16 percent compared to 34 per cent in the last parliamentary elections in 2016, when 66 per cent of registered voters voted.
“I have come to vote for my children, our place in Europe,” says Yosa Tanevski, a voter from Skopje, a 62-year-old.
Macedonian Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife, Zoritsa and his son Dusko, voted for a referendum in Macedonia to change the country's name, which will pave the way for its accession to NATO and the European Union in Strumitsa, Macedonia, on September 30, 2018.
In front of the parliamentary building in Skopje Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54 years old, prepared a small stage, placing chairs in traits established by those citizens who are going to boycott the referendum.
“We are in favor of membership in NATO and the European Union, but we want to unite with our heads held high, and not the upholstery of bureaucratic cabinets,” says Kavadarkov.
“Yes, we are citizens of a poor country, but we also have a sense of self-esteem.
If they don’t want to accept us as Macedonia, we can appeal to other countries such as China and Russia and become part of Euro-As-Asian integration.”
Prime Minister Zayev says that NATO membership will bring investments in Macedonia, which is so much necessary for this country, where the unemployment rate is more than 20 per cent.
“I believe that the vast majority will be in favor, because more than 80 percent of our citizens support the EU and NATO,” Zaev said after the vote.
He believes that the positive answer will be “the key to our prosperous future.”
A survey published last Monday by the Macedonian Institute of Political Studies found that between 30 and 43 per cent of voters would take part in a referendum, but this is lower than the required turnout.
In another survey, conducted by the Macedonian Canal Telecom-TV, it turned out that 57% of the respondents are planning to vote on Sunday.
70 per cent of this number say they will vote positively.
In order for a referendum to take place, the turnout must be 50 per cent plus one vote.
Failure of the referendum will be the first serious blow to the policy of the pro-Western government since it came to power in May of last year.
