These hopes may have become the most daring over the past decades, since Bolivia is awaiting the decision of an international court, which will take place on 1 October as a result of five years of the debate.
“Beavilia has the energy, the spirit of unity and peace of mind and, of course, are waiting for a positive result here,” the Bolivian diplomat Roberto Kalzadilla said.
Many Bolivians will observe the process of rendering a decision at the International Big-Clood Court throughout the country, hoping that the tribunal in The Hague will be able to support Bolibia's statement that, after decades of intense negotiations, Chile has an obligation to negotiably provide Bolivia with a sovereign landlock.
