JOHN Eduard: Knowledge of languages is of great importance to the citizens of the world
Inhabited schools of Scotland continue the tranquity of brilliant education, which was once again confirmed in 2018: the outstanding results of the exams demonstrated individual and collective successes in sports, the arts, music, and other socially significant disciplines.
These schools are part of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), more than 30 000 pupils all over Scawn are enthusiastic, here they strive to provide a higher level of services for pupils and their parents.
Independent schools strive to prepare their pupils for the further achievement of higher education, their chosen career, as well as the status of citizens of the world.
Being part of the educational sector, which is responsible for the development and implementation of an individual school curricula, we can see that modern languages continue to be a popular and desirable subject of choice in schools.
Nelson Mandela said: “If you’re talking to a man in a language that he understands, you appeal to his mind.
If you speak to him in his native language, then you turn to your heart.”
It’s conclusive evidence that we can’t only rely on English if we want to build relations with citizens of other countries and be able to trust them.
According to the results of the recent exams of this year, we see that the languages occupy a leading position in league tables and have the highest score according to the results of exams in independent schools.
A total of 68 per cent of pupils who have studied foreign languages have received the highest rating “A”.
Evidence from 74 SCIS schools found that 72 per cent of pupils received “A” under Mandarin (Chine language), while 72 per cent of German studies, 69 per cent of those studying French and 63 per cent of Spanish learners also received “A”.
This proves that in independent schools of Scastria, foreign languages are undoubtedly considered vital for children and young people in the future.
At the present time, language subjects are at the same demand as the natural sciences, technology, engineering, and mathematician in the curricula of independent schools and other educational institutions.
A survey conducted by the UK Employment and Snétilla Compliance in 2014 found that 17 per cent had a shortage of language skills among the reasons that caused employers to fill vacant vacancies, 17 per cent accounted for a lack of language skills.
Thus, the language skills are increasingly becoming a guarantee of a successful future career for young people.
Given the fact that more promising working positions require the knowledge of languages, these skills are necessary in a globalized world.
Regardless of what profession the student will choose, if he learns a second language, in the future he will receive a real advantage, mastering another additional skill, which will be useful to him throughout his later life.
The opportunity to directly communicate with people from other countries automatically gives a person who knows several foreign languages competitive advantages.
According to the YouGov poll, which was attended by more than 4,400 adult Britons in 2013, 75 per cent weren’t able to speak a foreign language well enough to support the conversation, and French turned out to be the only language with a double-digit percentage – 15 per cent of respondents say.
That's why investing in language education is so relevant now.
Evidence of several languages, in particular the languages of emerging-growing economies, will give their children a better chance to find a good job.
There’s a big scatter in Scotland’s schools that are taught – every school has its own set of languages.
A number of schools are concentrated in the more classical modern languages, while others teach languages that are considered the most important for the U.C. in the light of the country's future development until 2020 - such as Mandarin or Japanese.
No matter what the interests of your child, in independent schools there will always be a choice from several languages with teaching staff, whose members are specialists in these languages.
Industrious schools in Scots strive to create an educational environment that will train children and equip them with the skills necessary to achieve success, no matter what future awaits them.
At the present time it cannot be Denied that in the global business environment, languages remain vital in terms of the future development of the country, so this should appropriately be reflected in education.
In fact, modern languages really need to be regarded as “skills of international communication”.
Industrious schools will continue to offer this choice, which provides diversity and advantages for Scottish youth.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edvards, Director of the Council of Independent Schools
