While Ari repeated North Korean complaints of Washington’s “phased” approach to de-nuclearization, according to which North Korea will be rewarded for gradual steps, his statement seemed important in the sense that it didn’t reject unilateral denuclearization without control, as Pyongyang had done in the past.
Ri referred to the joint statement made by Kim Jong-un and Donald trump at the first-in-harden summit between the incumbent U.S. president and the North Korean leader, which took place in Singapore on June 12; at that time Kim promised to start moving towards “denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula”, and trump promised security guarantees to North Korea.
“North Korea seeks to officially end the 1950-53 Korean War, but the United States says Pyongyang must first abandon its nuclear weapons.
Washington also rekinds for calls to easing tough international sanctions against North Korea.
“The United States is pushing for “denuclearization must be carried out at first,” and increase the level of exertion through sanctions forcibly achieving its goal and even objecting to “announcement of the end of the war,” says Ari.
“Representing that sanctions can khaplet us is a utopian dream of people who don’t know us.
But the real problem is that ongoing sanctions increase our disbelliance.”
Ari did not mention the plans of the second summit between Kim and trump, which the U.S. leader mentioned at the beginning of the week at the beginning of the week.
Instead, the minister sharpened attention at the three encounters between Kim and South Korean leader Moon Jae-in in the last five months, and added: “If South Korea’s denuclearization was a party to this denuclearization issue, rather than the U.S., the denuclearization of the Korean peninsula wouldn’t be at such an impasse.”
Nevertheless, the tone of Rea’s speech differed sharply from last year, when he said at the U.N. General Assembly that the targeting of North Korea’s U.S. missile was unsupplied by the U.S. U.Short after “Mr. President of the Evil,” Trump described Kim as a “suicide missile.”
This year, at the United Nations, trump who last year jeopardized North Korya, praised Kim for his courage to debrief, but said that there is still a lot to be done, and the sanctions must remain in force until North Korea is disarmed.
On Wednesday, trump said that he does not outline any time limits for this process: “Whether it takes two years, for three years or five months does not matter.”
China and Russia believe that the UN Security Council should encourage Pyongyang for the steps that have been taken.
However, U.S. Secretary of state Mike Pompeo spoke in the UN Security Council on Thursday: “The imposition of sanctions by the Security Council must continue actively and necessarily until we see a complete, final and confirmed denuclearization.”
The Security Council unanimously tightened Sanctions against North Korea since 2006, aiming to ignite Pyongyang's nuclear and ballistic missile financing.
Pompeo has met with Ai on the sidelines of the United Nations General Assembly summit and said after this meeting that he will visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare for a second high-level meeting.
Pompeo visited North Korea this three times this year, but his last trip failed.
He left Pyongyang in July, saying that progress had been made, but within a few hours of the visit, North Korea denounced him for “gantry demands”.
“North Korea has vowed to dismantle the missile system, as well as a nuclear complex if the United States, for its part, takes “relevant measures”.
According to him, Kim told him that “corresponsive measures”, which he was talking about, are the security guarantees that trump promised in Singapore, as well as actions aimed at normalizing relations with Washington.
Harvard students learn to rest on special courses
A new course introduced this year at the University of Harvard helped all elementary students learn how to sleep more and deal with the chauvian culture of uninterrupted caffein consuming in order to awrestle “nrow at night”.
An academia found that the most successful students in the world often have no idea how to take care of themselves.
Charles Cheisler, a professor of sleep medicine at Harvard Medical School and a specialist at Brigham and Women's Hospital, has developed a course that he believes is the first of its kind in the U.S.
After his discovery was made, he caught the idea of introducing such a course, having previously made a presentation on the impact of lack of sleep on learning processes.
“At the end of my report, the girl approached me and said, “It’s a pity that I’ve only only found out when I’m almost graduating.”
“She said that no one has ever told her about the importance of sleep – what surprised me,” he told The Telegraph.
The course presented to students for the first time this year talks about how the habit of healthy sleep contributes to academic and athletic performance, as well as how it improves a person’s general well-feelability.
Paul Barreira, a professor of Psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and Executive Director of the University's Health Service, said the university decided to introduce this course after it was found that students experienced a serious lack of sleep during the academic week.
The lesson lasts one hour, the students perform a number of interactive tasks.
In one of the sections of the lesson, an image of a dormitory room is proposed, where students, clicking on coffee cups, curtains, simulators and books, receive information about how each of these actions and substances - coffee, the lights that cause a lack of sleep - affect the athletic performance, and how important it is to get a sufficient amount of sleep on a daily basis.
In another section, participants will find out about how long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risk of heart attacks, stroke, depression and cancer.
An interactivated campus map motivates course participants to think about their daily lives.
“We understand that these classes won’t change the behavior of students at once.
But we believe that they have the right to know, just like any other person has the right to be aware of, for example, how smoking influences his health, ”said Professor Cheisler.
According to him, the false sense of pride that a person can “plow all over the night” is still quite common, and modern technology, just as the increasing pressure on students means that deprivation of sleep becomes a problem.
According to him, a sufficient amount of good-quality sleep is a “secret weapon” of the student to combat stress, nervous exhaustion and anxiety, and even to deal with excess weight, since lack of sleep translates the brain into the mode of starvation, forcing him to constantly feel hungry.
Raymond So, a 19-year-largtly Califorian studying chemical and physical biology, helped Professor Cheysler develop a course by attending one lesson last year during his first-year at Harvard.
“He said the course opened his eyes to him and inspired him to disseminate this information throughout the campus.
He hopes that the next step - to ask all graduate students to pass a similar training program, before enrolling in a competitive educational institution.
Professor Chysler invites students to install an alarm clock to warn that you need to go to bed and waking, as well as to be aware of the harmful effects of “blue light”, emitted by electron screens and LED lamps, which breaks a person’s biorhythm and leads to problems falling asleep.
