Almanac: Inventor Gayger's vocaut
And now the page of our almanac “Sunrectury Morning”: September 30, 1882, 136 years ago the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm “Hanse” Geiger was born on this day in Germany.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity - an invention, which eventually led to the creation of a device known as the Geiger counter.
Having become one of the foundations of science, Geiger's meter also became one of the foundations of the popular culture, and this was reflected in the 1950-ies film "Coronado's Bells", with cowboy-like Roy Rogers and Dale Evans - such, it would seem, do not look like scientists - in their leading roles:
The person: “And what kind of thing is the thing like that?”
Rogers: "This Geiger counter is used to detect radioactive minerals like uranium.
When you put on these headphones, you can really hear the influence of atoms generated by radioactivity in the mineral.
Evans: “Oh, I’ll hear right now.”
Geiger's Hans has passed away in 1945 until his age.
But his inculcation, named in honor of its author, is still widely used.
