HIV/Ja: China reports 14 per cent increase in new cases
“China announced a 14 per cent increase in the number of its citizens who are infected with HIV/AIDS.
According to representatives of health services, in the country more than 820 000 people are affected by these diseases.
Only in the second quarter of 2018 there were about 40 000 new cases.
In the vast majority of new cases of the disease were sexually transmitted, which indicates a change in the trends of the past.
Traditionally, HIV has spread rapidly in some parts of China as a result of transfusing contaminated blood.
However, the number of infections of HIV in this way was practically reduced to zero, representatives of Chinese health officials said at a conference in Yunnan province.
Every year the number of people infected with HIV and AIDS in China increases by 100 000 people.
The transmission of HIV by sex is an alarming problem in the LGBT community of China.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but LGBT discrimination is still widespread.
Studies are corroborated that due to the conscientious sanctuaries, 70-90 per cent of men who have sexual intercourse with men will eventually marry women.
In many cases, the transmission of diseases occurs due to the lack of protection of these sexual minorities.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has pledged universal access to medicines against HIV as part of an effort to address this problem.
