The entry into operation of the new warship of Germany was again postponed
The latest frigate of the German navy was to be put into operation in 2014 to replace aerigating warship warship of the Cold War era, but this will not happen at least next year due to malforming systems and an avaluable increase in value, according to local media reports.
Reinland-Palatinate, the main ship of completely new Baden-Württemberg frigate classes, is now postponed until the first half of 2019, Die Zeit, citing a military representative.
The vessel was supposed to join the navy in 2014, but the problems that have arisen after the delivery have complicated the fate of an ambitious project.
Four Baden-Wüürtemberg class vessels commissioned by the nali navy in 2007 will be a replacement for aging Bremen class frigate.
As far as we know, they will have a powerful cannon, a lot of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, as well as some “steel” technologies – such as reduced radar, infrared and acoustic sacrificial siblings.
Other important characteristics include longer periods of maintenance - it should be possible to deploy the latest frigates for up to a period of up to a period of up to a period of up to a period of up to two years away from home ports.
Nevertheless, the constant postponement of commissioning means that the most modern warships, which are said to allow Germany to spread its power abroad will become obsolete by the time they enter into service.
Illuminous frigate F125 came into headline last year, when the German navy officially renounced the commissioning of the vessel and returned it to the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
This became the first case when the fleet returned the ship to the shipbuilder after delivery.
Fahnum was not known about the reasons for the return, but the German media referred to a number of “software and hardware flaws”, which made the warship fussy when used in battle.
Disadvantages of the software were especially critical, since Baden-Württemberg class vessels will be operated by a crew of about 120 seafarers - only half of the workforce on old Bremen class frigates.
In addition, it turned out that the ship is too heavy, which reduces its performance and limits the Navy’s ability to innovate in the future.
It is believed that the 7000-ton "Reinland-Palatinate" is twice heavier than ships of a similar class, which were used by the Germans in World War II.
In addition to malfunctioning equipment, the cost of the entire project, including the training of the team, also becomes a problem.
It is said that it has reached a staggering 3.1 billion euros (3.6 billion dollars), compared with the original 2.2 billion euros.
The problems of holding the latest frigates at anchor are becoming especially important in the light of the recent warnings about the reduction of Germany’s naval power.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, the head of the defense committee of the German parliament, admitted that the naval forces actually “expands the stock of ships capable of deployment.”
The official said that the problem escalated over time because the old ships were decommissioned, but they were not shipped to replace.
He lamented that none of Baden-Württemberg class frigates could not be taken into service by the fleet.
