Earthquakes and tsunami in Indonesia
According to officials, at least 384 people were killed in a powerful earthquake and tsunami that struck the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday and the death toll is expected to rise.
Due to the dissolution of communications, rescuers cannot obtain any information from Donggal, an area north of Palu, which is closer to the epicenter of an Earthquoia with a force of 7.5 points.
More than 16 000 people were evacuated in Palu as a result of a natural disaster.
Here are some key facts about Palu and Donggal, located on the island of Sulawesi:
Palu is the capital of Central Sulawesi province, located at the end of a narrow bay on the western coast of the island of Sulawesi, with an estimated population of 379,800 (data for in 2017).
The city celebled its 40-anniversary, when an earthquake and a tsunami took place.
Donggala is an area stretching along 300 km (180 ile) of the coastline in the north-west of the island of Sulawesi.
The county, an administrative district is smaller than the province, in 2017 numbered 2,99 200 people.
Fisheries and agriculture form the backbone of Central Sulawesi's economy, in particular the coastal region of Donggala.
Nickel presidence is also important for the province’s economy, but is mainly concentrated in Morovali, on the opposite coast of Sulavesi.
According to the Indonesian Agency for the Elimination of the Consequences of Natural Disasters, over the past 100 years the tsunami several times inflicted destruction in Palu and Donggal.
In 1938 the tsunami killed more than 200 people and demolished hundreds of houses in Donggala.
Tsunami also took place in the western part of Donggal in 1996, as a result, nine people were killed.
Indonesia is located in the Pacific Volcanic Rycardency Ring and regularly suffers from an Earthquois.
Here are some of the major Earthquake and tsunami in recent years:
2004: A strong earthquake on the west coast of the Indonesian province of Aceh in northern Sumatra on December 26 caused a tsunami that hit 14 countries, which killed 226 000 people along the coast of the Indian Ocean, more than half of them in Achih.
“Sequence of strong quarrels has struck the west coast of Sumatra in late mid-April.
Hundreds of people were killed on the island of Nias, off the coast of Sumatra.
2006: An Earthquoise with a force of 6.8 erupted south of Yava, the most populous island of Indonesia, causing a tsunami that hit the southern coast, which resulted in the deaths of about 700 people.
2009: An Earthquake of magnitude 7.6 took place in Padang, West Sumatra province.
More than 1100 people were killed.
2010: An Earthquoise with a force of 7.5 points took place on one of the Mentavai islands near Sumatra, causing a tsunami of up to 10 meter, which destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: Weak Earthquappe hit Pedi-Jaya district in Aceh, provocating and correcting, as people remembered the devastating earthquake of 2004 and tsunami.
This time the tsunami did not take place, but more than 100 people died due to the destruction of buildings.
2018: Strengest earthquakes have hit the tourist island of Indonesia Lombok, resulting in killing more than 500 people, mostly on the northern side of the island.
An Earthquake destroyed thousands of buildings, and thousands of tourists temporarily remained under the ruins.
