The Federal Prosecutor’s office wants to make a suspect in the terrorist attack in new York a rare death sentence
The Federal Prosecutor’s office in New York seeks the death penalty for Saifullo Saipov, suspected of carrying out a terrorist attack in New York, which killed eight people – a rar punishment for a federal offense that hasn’t been used in the state since 1953.
In Occasion Saipov, for 30 years, supposedly rented a Home Depot truck to carry an attack on a bicycle track on the West Side highway in Lower Manhattan, knocking pedestrians and cyclists down.
To acquiescuse the veripulation, prosecutors will have to prove that Saipov “intentionally” killed eight people and “intentionally” caused serious bodily injuries, according to the notice of intent to achieve the death penalty filed in the Southern district of New York.
According to the court document, on these two accusations, a death sentence may be handed down.
A few weeks after the attack, a large federal jury retaliated against Saipov with 22 counts, which included eight racket homicide charges commonly used by federal prosecutors in organized crime cases, as well as accusations of violence and the destruction of cars.
According to investigators, the attack was “carefully planned and premeditated”, Saipov carried it out, as described, “distinct, cruel and criminally”.
“Sifullo Khabibullaevich Saipov caused injuries, caused damage and loss to the families and friends of Diego Henrique Angelini, Nicolas Cleves, Anne-Lor Decadat, Darren Drake, Ariel Eryl Erylia, Hernán Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alehandro Damian Pagnukko.
The five people killed were tourists from Argentina.
It's been ten years since the prosecutor demanded the death penalty in the southern district of New York for a criminal offense.
In September 2009, accused Khalid Barnes was sustorized for the murder of two drug delinders but was eventually sentenced to life in prison.
For the last time the death penalty in a federal case in New York was applied in 1953 against Julius and Ethel Rosenberg, a married vapor executed for conspir Circulation for espionage in favor of the Soviet Union during the Cold War two years earlier.
The Rosenbergs were executed using an electric stool on June 19, 1953.
Saipov, according to court documents, a native of Uzbekistan, demonstrated the absence of remorse after an attack.
According to the police, he told investigators that he was happy that he had done it.
According to the instigator, Saipov told the authorities that he was inspired to watch a video of ISIS on a phone.
He also asked to hike an ISIS flag in his hospital pilgrimage, police said.
He denies his guilt on all 22 indictments.
David Patton, one of Saipov's federal public defenders, said they were "clearly frustrated" by the prosecutor's decision.
“We believe that the decision to apply the death penalty, and not a life-of-execution without the possibility of releasing will only exacerbate the pain of these events for all their participants,” Patton said.
Saipov’s defense has previously appealed to prosecutors with a request not to achieve the death penalty.
